<h2>The petition and its urgency</h2>
<p>The petition was moved under Article 226 of the Constitution read with Section 528 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, by a girl of about 17 years, acting through her grandmother. An FIR stood registered at Police Station Khyala in respect of the sexual assault from which the pregnancy resulted, and the minor was under the care of Deen Dayal Upadhyay Hospital. By the time the matter reached the Court, the pregnancy had advanced to roughly 29 weeks — well past the 24-week ceiling that Section 3(2) of the Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act, 1971 sets for registered medical practitioners acting on their own authority.</p>
<p>Both the minor and her grandmother were unequivocal: continuation of the pregnancy would inflict serious mental injury and lasting psychological trauma. A Medical Board constituted at the hospital examined her and reported that she was physically fit for the procedure, and, in the Board’s words, <em>"medically &amp; psychologically fit for termination of pregnancy."</em></p>

<h2>Why the 24-week ceiling did not conclude the matter</h2>
<p>The Court’s central holding is that a case of this kind <strong>"cannot be merely determined or restricted by the statutory period of 24 weeks."</strong> The statutory scheme guides registered medical practitioners; it does not exhaust the jurisdiction of a constitutional court dealing with the fundamental rights of the pregnant person under Article 21. The Court identified the considerations that actually govern: the age of the pregnant person, the manner in which the pregnancy was conceived, the medical opinion placed on record, and the rights, interests and expressed will of the person carrying the pregnancy.</p>
<p>The judgment draws on the Supreme Court’s recent affirmation that constitutional courts may permit termination beyond the statutory ceiling where the welfare, dignity and reproductive autonomy of the woman so require, quoting the principle that <em>"no court ought to compel any woman and more so a minor child, to carry a pregnancy to full term against her express will."</em> It also invokes coordinate-bench decisions of the Delhi High Court permitting terminations at stages ranging from 24 to 33 weeks. The doctrinal footing is summed up in one line of the judgment: <strong>"a lack of remedy under a statute does not bar a constitutional remedy."</strong></p>

<h2>The directions</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h4>1. Permission</h4><p>Termination of the pregnancy was permitted in the minor’s best interest, to be carried out by competent doctors in accordance with the MTP Act, the Rules and prescribed guidelines.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>2. Records</h4><p>The Medical Board must maintain a complete record of the procedure performed.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>3. Evidence preservation</h4><p>Foetal tissue is to be preserved for DNA testing, so that the termination does not impair the investigation and trial of the offence.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>4. Costs on the State</h4><p>All expenses of the termination, the hospital stay and post-operative care are to be borne by the State.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>5. Live-birth contingency</h4><p>If the child is born alive, medical support must be extended and the Child Welfare Committee informed; adoption, if desired, follows the prescribed procedure.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>6. Identity protection</h4><p>No detail capable of identifying the minor — name, address, photographs or medical records — may be disclosed except as authorised by law.</p></div>
</div>
<p>A copy of the order was directed to be sent to the SHO and the Investigating Officer of Police Station Khyala, who is to communicate it to the treating medical officers.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters in practice</h2>
<p>For practitioners advising survivors and their families, three features of the judgment deserve attention. First, <strong>speed matters more than form</strong>: these petitions are heard on a writ footing precisely because every week of delay narrows the medical options, and courts in Delhi routinely constitute or act upon Medical Board reports within days. Second, the Medical Board’s opinion is the fulcrum — a report addressing both physical fitness and psychological state gives the court the material on which permission beyond 24 weeks can rest. Third, the standard directions now form a recognisable template: preservation of foetal tissue for the criminal case, State-borne costs, the live-birth protocol and strict identity protection travel together with the permission.</p>
<p>The judgment also illustrates the interface between the criminal process and medical law. The FIR and the ongoing investigation did not merely form the background; they shaped the relief, with the Court ensuring that the DNA evidence relevant to the prosecution survives the procedure. The writ court thus protected the survivor’s autonomy without compromising the trial that is to come.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>Petitions of this nature are heard with the urgency they deserve, but the paperwork must be ready on day one: the FIR, the medico-legal record, proof of age, the guardian’s authorisation where the survivor is a minor, and any existing medical opinion. A complete file is often the difference between an order on the first listing and a week lost to a fresh Medical Board reference.</p>
</div>
